# Agentic CI/CD: Kubernetes Deployment Gate with Elastic MCP Server

A CI/CD pipeline that blocks a Kubernetes deployment automatically when cluster
resource usage is unhealthy — using an AI agent (Elastic Agent Builder) exposed
over MCP as the health-check gate, instead of static scripted checks.

Built entirely on free-tier tooling: a local Kind cluster, a self-hosted GitHub
Actions runner, and an Elastic Cloud free trial project.

## Architecture

```
 GitHub push
      │
      ▼
 [build-and-push]  (GitHub-hosted runner)
   - build Docker image, tag with commit SHA
   - push to Docker Hub
      │
      ▼
 [k8s-health-gate]  (self-hosted runner)
   - calls Elastic Agent Builder tool `k8s_node_health_check` via MCP
   - tool runs an ES|QL query against live OTel metrics in Elasticsearch
   - parses memory_usage_pct, compares to threshold (80%)
   - exits 1 (blocks pipeline) if unhealthy
      │  (only if gate passes)
      ▼
 [deploy]  (self-hosted runner)
   - updates image tag in k8s/deployment.yaml
   - commits + pushes to git
      │
      ▼
 ArgoCD (auto-sync, in-cluster) reconciles the Kind cluster
```

Data path for the gate itself:

```
Kind cluster → OTel Collector (kubeletstats receiver, daemonset)
            → OTLP export → Elastic Cloud (Elasticsearch project)
            → ES|QL query → Agent Builder Tool → exposed via MCP
            → called by GitHub Actions step
```

## Stack

| Component | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Kubernetes cluster | Kind (local) | Zero cost vs. EKS's continuous control-plane charge |
| GitOps / deploy | ArgoCD | Real automated sync from git, not a scripted `kubectl apply` |
| Metrics collection | OpenTelemetry Operator + Collector (daemonset, `kubeletstats` receiver) | Standard OTel path to Elastic |
| Observability backend | Elastic Cloud (free trial, general "Elasticsearch" project type) | Free Agent Builder execution quota (1,000/mo on this project type) |
| Health-check logic | ES|QL query wrapped as an Agent Builder Tool | Reusable, testable independent of the agent |
| Agent | Agent Builder Agent (`kubernetes_analysis_agent`) | Demonstrates the "agentic" pattern conversationally |
| Exposure | Elastic's built-in MCP server | Lets any MCP client (GitHub Actions here) call the tool |
| CI/CD | GitHub Actions (hybrid: cloud + self-hosted runner) | Self-hosted leg needed to reach a local cluster + push git commits |
| Sample app | Minimal FastAPI service (`/`, `/health`) | The app being deployed is intentionally not the focus |

## Repo layout

```
agentic-cicd-gate/
├── app/
│   ├── main.py              # FastAPI app: / and /health
│   └── requirements.txt
├── Dockerfile
├── k8s/
│   └── deployment.yaml      # Deployment + NodePort Service (ArgoCD watches this path)
└── .github/
    └── workflows/
        └── deploy.yaml      # build-and-push → k8s-health-gate → deploy
```

## How the health gate works

1. An OTel Collector daemonset scrapes kubelet stats (`k8s.node.cpu.usage`,
   `k8s.node.memory.usage/available/working_set`) every 20s and exports them
   via OTLP to Elastic.
2. An ES|QL query aggregates per-node averages and computes
   `memory_usage_pct = working_set / (working_set + available) * 100`.
3. That query is registered as an Agent Builder **Tool** (`k8s_node_health_check`),
   independently testable in Kibana.
4. An **Agent** (`kubernetes_analysis_agent`) wraps the tool with a system prompt
   for conversational use (tested manually — both pass and fail thresholds verified).
5. For the actual CI/CD gate, GitHub Actions calls the **tool directly** over
   MCP (`tools/call`), not the agent's chat — this returns clean structured
   JSON instead of prose, so the pass/fail decision in the workflow is
   deterministic (`jq` + `bc`, threshold = 80% memory).
6. A failing check calls `exit 1`, which stops the GitHub Actions job — the
   `deploy` job (which depends on the gate job) never runs.

## Key setup notes / gotchas hit during the build

- **MCP requires an explicit `Accept` header.** Elastic's MCP endpoint returns
  `406 Not Acceptable` without `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream`
  on every request.
- **MCP tool responses are double-encoded JSON.** The top-level
  `result.content[0].text` field is itself a JSON string — parse it twice
  (`jq -r '.result.content[0].text' | jq ...`).
- **No `k8s.node.cpu.utilization` field.** Only raw `k8s.node.cpu.usage`
  (fractional cores) is emitted — there's no ready-made percentage, so the
  gate uses memory % as the hard threshold and reports CPU cores as
  informational context only.
- **GitHub-hosted runners can't reach a local cluster.** Solved with a
  self-hosted runner registered against the repo, running on the same
  machine as the Kind cluster.
- **`jq` and `bc` aren't preinstalled** on a fresh self-hosted runner —
  install them on the host before the workflow can parse/compare the
  MCP response.

## Required GitHub repo secrets

| Secret | Value |
|---|---|
| `DOCKERHUB_USERNAME` | Docker Hub username |
| `DOCKERHUB_TOKEN` | Docker Hub access token (not your account password) |
| `ELASTIC_MCP_ENDPOINT` | `https://<project>.kb.<region>.<provider>.elastic.cloud/api/agent_builder/mcp` |
| `ELASTIC_MCP_API_KEY` | Elastic API key scoped for MCP access |

## Local setup summary

```bash
# 1. Local cluster
kind create cluster --config kind-config.yaml

# 2. ArgoCD
kubectl create namespace argocd
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml

# 3. OTel Operator + Collector (see otel-rbac.yaml, otel-collector.yaml)
helm install opentelemetry-operator open-telemetry/opentelemetry-operator \
  -n opentelemetry-operator-system --create-namespace
kubectl apply -f otel-rbac.yaml
kubectl apply -f otel-collector.yaml

# 4. ArgoCD Application
kubectl apply -f argocd-app.yaml

# 5. Self-hosted GitHub Actions runner
cd actions-runner && ./run.sh
```

## Result

A push to `main` that touches `app/**` or `Dockerfile` triggers: build → push
to Docker Hub → live cluster health check via an AI agent's tool over MCP →
(if healthy) an automatic GitOps deploy through ArgoCD. Verified working
end-to-end, including a deliberate low-threshold test that correctly blocked
a deployment.

## Teardown

```bash
kind delete cluster --name agentic-cicd
```

Delete the Elastic Cloud project from the Kibana project dropdown → **Manage
this project** → **Delete project**. Remove the self-hosted runner via repo
**Settings → Actions → Runners**, and revoke the Docker Hub access token.